{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli who ruled from roughly 1192 to 1275 though his continuous active rule lasted from around 1197 until 1240. He is notable for his efforts in defending his territory against various threats particularly the forces of the Ayyubid dynasty and later the Mamluks. Raymond was the son of Raymond II and his wife Eschiva and succeeded his father as count upon the latter's death.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1: Childhood and Early Life*\nRaymond III was born around 1165 into the powerful House of Bures. His family held significant influence within the Crusader states especially in the County of Tripoli. His upbringing took place during a turbulent period marked by conflicts among local Crusader lords and the expanding power of the Muslim forces under Saladin. Raymond was likely educated to manage governance and military leadership reflecting the expectations placed upon the nobility of his time.\n\nUpon the death of his father Raymond II in 1192 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli. His early years as count were overshadowed by the power struggles within the family and the broader Crusader states. His uncle Raymond of Sidon contested his claim to the throne but eventually Raymond III consolidated his authority and established himself firmly as the ruling count by the late 1190s.\n\n*Highlighted section part 2: Reign and Military Campaigns*\nRaymond III’s reign was characterized by a series of military campaigns aimed at preserving and expanding the territorial integrity of Tripoli. One of his earliest significant engagements was the defense of his county against the Ayyubid forces led by Al-Adil I and later his son Al-Kamil. These conflicts were commonly marked by sieges and battles that tested Raymond’s strategic acumen and diplomatic skills.\n\nIn 1216 Raymond participated in the Fifth Crusade which targeted Egypt. Though the Crusade did not achieve its primary objective Raymond’s involvement highlighted his commitment to the larger Crusader cause. After the Crusade Raymond sought to stabilize his borders through diplomacy and fortified alliances particularly with other Crusader states like Antioch and Armenia.\n\nAnother pivotal event during his reign occurred in 1233 when Raymond laid siege to the city of Marqab which was held by the Ayyubids. The siege ended in triumph allowing Raymond to extend his control over additional territories in the region. His military prowess and successful campaigns earned him respect among his contemporaries and solidified his position as a prominent leader among the Crusader states.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3: Later Life and Legacy*\nTowards the end of his reign Raymond III faced new challenges including the rise of the Mamluks under Baybars who began to pose a more significant threat to the Crusader states. Despite his advanced age Raymond continued to demonstrate leadership particularly in the defense against Mamluk invasions. By the 1250s his health began to decline and he increasingly relied on his sons for day-to-day governance.\n\nRaymond III died around 1275 after a long and impactful reign. His contributions to the defense and expansion of the County of Tripoli were substantial and his efforts played a key role in the survival of the Crusader states during a period of intense conflict. Upon his death he was succeeded by his son Bohemond VI who continued the legacy of resistance against the advancing Muslim powers.\n\nRaymond III is remembered as one of the more capable and enduring counts of Tripoli. His ability to navigate complex political and military landscapes made him an important figure in the history of the Crusades. Modern historical accounts often highlight his strategic decisions and his enduring efforts to protect Christian rule in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.07479121536016464}, {"toke